{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794886319,"nonce":"kzMpzygr4P17f9e6Gl0e6/jJw74=","title":"Reported 2026-11-17 from a 0.4.1 install (tools-common, single replica, image 0.4.1@sha256:ce21bf8a…).","message":"Replica started 2026-11-15T10:43Z. Its `leases` rows `cron-scheduler`, `retention-sweeper`, `cas-gc` kept renewing (term 10, expires_at ~1 min ahead). `scheduler` stayed owned by the same replica, term 10, but `expires_at` froze at 2026-11-16T20:21:13Z (last renewal ~20:20Z). Nothing logged at RUST_LOG=info: no panic, error or lease-lost line; /readyz kept passing. Only nearby WARNs: two `slow statement ... COMMIT` (1.45s, 1.13s) at 19:42:16Z (and the same pattern the day before at 19:42:12Z).\n\nEffect: webhooks answered 202 with run ids, runs sat `pending` (creating=f) for hours, no commit status, no step Pod. Runs 01a1e77b-21f7…, 01a1e77b-2249… (PR 2949), 01a1e781-7ac7…, 01a1e781-7afe… (PR 2981). `kubectl rollout restart` at 03:30Z: the new replica took `scheduler` and all pending runs started within a minute.\n\n- The `scheduler` lease has no heartbeat of its own: `Scheduler::tick_all` renews it inline (`leader_term`, crates/scarab-engine/src/scheduler.rs ~2658), once per driver cycle.\n- The driver (`converged::spawn_driver`, crates/scarab-server/src/converged.rs ~230) is a bare `tokio::spawn` loop: `tick_once` (tick_all, log tails, infra observation, forge status drain) with NO deadline around it. Fenced leader transactions are bounded in Postgres (`begin_fenced`: lock/statement/idle timeouts), but unfenced reads (`active_runs`, `unfinished_creations_before`, the lease upsert itself), executor (kube API) calls and the forge drain have no per-call bound here. Any await that never returns freezes the whole cycle, so the lease stops renewing while the other lease loops (separate tasks) keep going.\n- A panic in the driver task ends it; `main` keeps the JoinHandle only to abort it at shutdown, so a dead driver goes unnoticed too.\n- /readyz checks only a DB query (`run_status_counts`).\n- On a single replica nobody takes the expired lease over, so nothing runs until a restart.\n\n1. Bound each cycle: `tokio::time::timeout` around `tick_once` (e.g. a few × lease TTL), WARN naming the cycle on expiry, and per-leg timing at debug so the stuck leg can be found.\n2. Driver heartbeat: record the last completed cycle; a liveness endpoint (or /readyz) fails when this replica owns `scheduler` and has not completed a cycle within N × TTL, so Kubernetes restarts it.\n3. Supervise the driver JoinHandle: if it ends, log ERROR and exit the process.\n4. Investigate what blocked ~20:20Z: candidates are a kube API call with no timeout, a forge HTTP call, or a DB read waiting on a lock outside a fenced transaction (the 19:42 slow COMMITs suggest DB pressure but are 40 min earlier).","files":null}]}